from author import *
unit(0,'risk-contract','Define an AI release contract',
'''An AI feature is a system of deterministic boundaries around probabilistic inference. A useful release contract separates hard constraints from graded usefulness: a tool must never read another tenant’s logs, while a summary may be correct in several phrasings. Model quality cannot compensate for an unauthorized action.\n\nWrite a risk register before selecting a framework. Identify who can be harmed, the action scope, evidence sources, uncertainty tolerance, and fallback. An AI quality engineer collaborates on these product decisions rather than merely choosing an evaluator after implementation.''',
'''Represent each invariant as an executable predicate and each graded requirement as a documented rubric. Version the dataset, prompt, model, retriever, and policy with the run. Compare matched cases and retain per-case evidence instead of storing only an average.\n\nA project README should distinguish observations on synthetic data from production claims. Define who adjudicates uncertain outputs and how a release can be rolled back. The risk contract becomes acceptance criteria for the learner’s portfolio.''',
'''A score of 92% can hide a single severe cross-tenant failure. Segment the report by risk and prohibit aggregation from overriding critical gates. A model refusal is not automatically correct: an authorized, answerable request may have been refused unnecessarily.\n\nReproduce the request using its exact versions, check the authorization decision, and inspect evidence passed to the model. Determine whether the defect is a broken deterministic boundary or an uncertain response.''',
'''Build a small suite with an allowed request, a forbidden request, an unanswerable question, and a valid paraphrase. Record contract compliance separately from usefulness. Show which boundary prevented a forbidden action and which rubric scored the paraphrase.\n\nThe release review should include failures, confidence limits, and operating budgets. An SDET’s regression mindset becomes especially valuable when every model upgrade can change behavior without changing application code.''',
'checks = {"schema_valid": True, "tenant_allowed": False, "quality": 0.92}\nrelease = checks["schema_valid"] and checks["tenant_allowed"]\nprint(release)',
rubric('Design the release contract for a CI-log summarizer that can access two synthetic tenants. Include hard gates, quality evidence, and an outage fallback.',[{'label':'Tenant boundary','terms':['tenant','authorization']},{'label':'Measured quality','terms':['dataset','rubric']},{'label':'Recovery','terms':['fallback','rollback']}], 'Authorize tenant access before retrieval, validate the response schema, and reject forbidden tool proposals. Evaluate grounded summaries on a versioned dataset using a calibrated rubric. On outage return a deterministic evidence-only report and retain rollback versions.'),
('A high average score hides a cross-tenant read. What wins?',['The mean score','The hard authorization gate','The longest answer'],1,'Authorization is a release constraint independent of averaged quality.'))
unit(1,'async','Bounded async, cancellation, and retry',
'''Async functions suspend at await so other tasks can progress during I/O. They do not turn CPU-heavy parsing into parallel computation. Blocking a model SDK or file transform inside an async route can still stall every concurrent request.\n\nConcurrency should be a budget: use a semaphore to bound outstanding API calls and define a deadline for the whole operation. A generator yields records lazily; an async generator can stream observations without retaining an entire report.''',
'''Use async with for locks and resource lifetimes. asyncio.wait_for bounds a coroutine and cancels it on timeout; propagate cancellation rather than catching it as an ordinary successful result. Retry only classified transient errors within the original deadline.\n\nDecorators can add timing while functools.wraps preserves names and metadata. Keep retry/timing wrappers separate from business transformation. Test both the returned values and whether cancellation closes the underlying resource.''',
'''Unbounded gather over thousands of CI logs can exhaust sockets and trigger rate limits. A synchronous sleep in an async retry blocks the event loop; use asyncio.sleep. Repeated retries with a fresh timeout can exceed the user’s overall request budget.\n\nInject a fake clock or zero delay and a fake transport for tests. Count attempts, assert a permanent error is not retried, and check cancellation leaves no background write. Do not retry a non-idempotent action without an idempotency contract.''',
'''Exercise a bounded fetcher with slow, successful, transient, and permanent responses. Measure maximum simultaneous calls, total deadline, and abandoned work. Testing concurrency is about observable contracts rather than an exact scheduler order.\n\nThis applies to Playwright API checks, batched log analysis, provider requests, and future agent tools. A good interview explanation names the independent I/O work and the enforced concurrency budget.''',
'import asyncio\nasync def collect(items, fetch, limit=2):\n    sem = asyncio.Semaphore(limit)\n    async def one(item):\n        async with sem:\n            return await asyncio.wait_for(fetch(item), 2)\n    return await asyncio.gather(*(one(x) for x in items))',
py('Implement retryable(status): retry only 429 and 500–599. A 400 validation failure must not retry.','def retryable(status):\n    return status == 429 or 500 <= status <= 599','assert retryable(429) and retryable(503)\nassert not retryable(400) and not retryable(200)', 'def retryable(status):\n    return False'),
('Which operation blocks an async event loop?',['await asyncio.sleep(1)','time.sleep(1)','Awaiting a socket read'],1,'Synchronous sleep prevents other tasks on the loop from progressing.'))
unit(1,'models','Classes, enums, Pydantic, and contract testing',
'''Classes group state and behavior; a dataclass is convenient for an internal record, and an enum restricts named domain choices. Inheritance can model a stable substitutable interface, but composition is usually clearer for a transport, validator, and reporter.\n\nStatic types assist review and tools. External JSON still needs runtime validation. Pydantic models can enforce bounds and forbid extra fields; strict behavior must be selected deliberately because coercion can otherwise accept a string where a number was intended.''',
'''Create a request model with a bounded log, explicit category enum, and strict duration. Use model_validate at the API boundary and model_dump for serialization. Use field validators for rules that involve more than basic types.\n\nIn tests, parameterize valid and invalid requests. A fixture constructs a new service per test; monkeypatch replaces environment values without leaking them to the next test. Mock an adapter protocol so schema validation and business logic remain real.''',
'''A unit mock that returns prevalidated domain objects bypasses the dangerous parsing boundary. Include integration cases with missing keys, extra keys, negative values, booleans in numeric fields, and invalid enums. Property tests verify invariants over generated records.\n\nA marker can separate network integration tests, but excluding all of them forever is not a quality strategy. Know which tests ran, and retain one meaningful adapter contract check with synthetic payloads.''',
'''Keep request, response, and internal state models distinct so a response cannot leak private transport fields. Test serialization and exception mapping as part of the public API contract. Demonstrate a fixture’s cleanup with a failing test.\n\nThis pattern transfers directly to REST/GraphQL contract testing and structured AI output. A valid schema is necessary, but it cannot establish that a predicted defect category is factually correct.''',
'from enum import Enum\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, ConfigDict, Field\nclass Status(str, Enum):\n    passed="passed"\n    failed="failed"\nclass Result(BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra="forbid")\n    name: str = Field(min_length=1)\n    status: Status\n    duration: float = Field(ge=0, strict=True)',
rubric('Propose tests for a Pydantic result API that currently accepts unknown status strings and leaked internal fields.',[{'label':'Boundary inputs','terms':['enum','extra']},{'label':'Runtime enforcement','terms':['validation','strict']},{'label':'Behavior isolation','terms':['fixture','mock']}], 'Use enum categories, strict bounded duration validation, and extra=forbid. Parameterize malformed JSON and missing/extra fields. Use fresh fixtures and mock only the external transport; test real validation and response serialization.'),
('Does a schema-valid prediction prove a correct classification?',['Yes','No; compare with labeled evidence','Only with strict validation'],1,'Validation establishes structure, while correctness requires domain evidence.'))
unit(1,'files','Files, structured logs, and streaming reports',
'''pathlib makes path composition explicit across operating systems. JSON preserves nested records; CSV represents rows and needs a defined dialect, schema, and encoding. Environment variables configure processes without committing secrets.\n\nA context manager owns cleanup even when parsing fails. Iterators and generators let a result analyzer process large histories incrementally, but consumers must understand that a stream is exhausted after iteration.''',
'''Separate reading from transformation and writing. Open files with encoding=utf-8 and newline="" for CSV. Validate rows as they are read and retain rejected-row counts. Emit JSON logs containing event, run_id, stage, and outcome; redact log bodies and credentials.\n\nWrite reports atomically: create a temporary sibling file, flush it, then replace the final path. A crash should leave either the previous complete report or the new one, rather than a partially written artifact.''',
'''A relative path can refer to a different directory in CI than in VS Code. Anchor fixture paths to the repository or require an explicit CLI argument. Logging the entire environment can expose provider credentials.\n\nA reused generator can produce an empty second metric pass. Aggregate in one pass or deliberately materialize bounded input. Test a corrupt row halfway through a stream and verify cleanup and error reporting.''',
'''Build a CLI report containing accepted rows, rejected rows, failure frequency, and duration summaries. Make exit status nonzero for violated ingestion policy so GitHub Actions can block the pipeline. Keep ordinary empty input distinguishable from a failed read.\n\nThe same boundaries support dataset ingestion, evaluation artifacts, and incident reports. An SDET should be able to reproduce an artifact from a clean checkout and explain every excluded record.''',
'import csv\nfrom pathlib import Path\ndef rows(path):\n    with Path(path).open(encoding="utf-8", newline="") as stream:\n        yield from csv.DictReader(stream)\n# list(rows("datasets/test-runs.csv"))',
py('Implement count_status(rows): return status counts in a single pass over any iterable. Do not iterate twice.','def count_status(rows):\n    counts = {}\n    for row in rows:\n        key = row["status"]\n        counts[key] = counts.get(key, 0) + 1\n    return counts','assert count_status(iter([{"status":"failed"},{"status":"passed"},{"status":"failed"}])) == {"failed":2,"passed":1}\nassert count_status(iter([])) == {}','def count_status(rows):\n    return {}'),
('What does an exhausted generator produce on the next pass?',['Its original rows','No rows','A fresh file read'],1,'A generator tracks iteration state and cannot automatically restart.'))
unit(3,'pivot','Pivot tables, UTC time, and leakage-safe features',
'''A pivot table turns row observations into a labeled matrix after aggregation. Choose index, columns, values, and aggregation explicitly: module-by-build first-attempt failure rate differs from test-by-date mean duration. Missing cells mean no observation, not zero failures.\n\nParse timestamps to timezone-aware UTC before sorting or resampling. Daylight-saving changes and mixed local offsets can create ambiguous ordering. Keep categorical identifiers as categories only when their vocabulary and unknown-value policy are defined.''',
'''Use pd.to_datetime(..., utc=True), sort by test and timestamp, then group and shift to build lagged features. Merge metadata with validate="many_to_one". A rolling feature for prediction must use observations strictly before the predicted run.\n\nPrefer vectorized transforms and map for straightforward substitutions; apply is useful for complex row logic but can be slower and conceal type changes. Inspect dtypes after transformations and keep raw evidence immutable.''',
'''A rolling window that includes the current outcome predicts itself. Filtering away tests with no recent runs can bias a report toward healthy frequently executed tests. Imputing absent modules with zero failure rate gives invented evidence.\n\nInspect the feature’s timestamp against the target timestamp. Check join multiplication, unmatched keys, and whether retries have been mistaken for separate test identities. Compare segment-level duration distributions before labeling an overall slowdown.''',
'''Build a CI health report with per-module counts, first-attempt pass rates, mixed-outcome candidates, UTC daily trends, and rejected records. Supply a small hand-calculated fixture to verify the pivot and lag behavior.\n\nFeature engineering for the failure classifier should use only what was known when the run failed. Report the forecast horizon and validation split so another engineer can reproduce the apparent gain.''',
'import pandas as pd\ndf=pd.DataFrame({"test":["a","a","b"],"duration":[2.,4.,1.],"build":[1,2,1]})\nprint(df.pivot_table(index="test",columns="build",values="duration",aggfunc="mean"))\nprint(df.sort_values(["test","build"]).groupby("test")["duration"].shift(1))',
py('Implement previous_duration(df): sort by test then build; return the prior duration for each sorted row, replacing missing prior values with None.','def previous_duration(df):\n    s=df.sort_values(["test","build"]).groupby("test")["duration"].shift(1)\n    return [None if __import__("pandas").isna(v) else float(v) for v in s]','import pandas as pd\ndf=pd.DataFrame({"test":["a","b","a"],"build":[2,1,1],"duration":[4.,1.,2.]})\nassert previous_duration(df)==[None,2.,None]','import pandas as pd\ndef previous_duration(df):\n    return []'),
('Which feature avoids leaking the target outcome?',['Current failure status','Previous build duration','Resolved defect label'],1,'Lagged observations can be available before the target, unlike its outcome.'))
unit(4,'query-plans','Subqueries, CASE, UNION, and query plans',
'''A correlated subquery references an outer row and can express existence without multiplying it. EXISTS asks whether at least one related row matches; a join returns all matching pairs. Choose based on the desired cardinality, then inspect the actual plan.\n\nCASE models conditional expressions, UNION deduplicates result rows, and UNION ALL preserves events. Deduplication is not a repair for an incorrect join. Relational modeling separates stable test identity, builds, executions, and failure annotations.''',
'''Use EXPLAIN to inspect operators and EXPLAIN ANALYZE when it is safe to execute the query. Examine filters, estimated and actual rows, join cardinality, and scan cost. Index usefulness depends on the engine and workload; do not assume an OLTP B-tree rule applies unchanged to DuckDB analytical scans.\n\nFor consecutive build failures, first reduce retries to a documented build-level outcome. Then LAG and LEAD operate over the build sequence. Require adjacency rather than merely three failed rows.''',
'''An inner join drops unknown test metadata. A many-to-many join fabricates additional failure events. Selecting MAX(status) does not select the status associated with the latest timestamp.\n\nConstruct a fixture with duplicate metadata, missing builds, and multiple attempts. Compare row counts after every CTE. Query performance diagnoses should explain work performed, not just whether a query appears short.''',
'''Verify a regression query against hand-calculated expected output and test tie ordering. Include the actual SQL, schema, sample count, and plan in the CI report. Use separate correctness and performance gates so a fast wrong query cannot pass.\n\nSQL interviews reward explicit NULL and cardinality reasoning. In an AI evaluation store, incorrect joins can produce an apparent model improvement by silently dropping difficult cases.''',
"SELECT tc.name FROM test_cases tc\nWHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM test_runs tr\n              WHERE tr.test_id=tc.test_id AND tr.status='failed')\nORDER BY tc.name;",
dict(kind='sql',prompt='Return name for test cases with any failed execution. Sort by name. Use EXISTS to avoid multiplication.',answer='',solution="SELECT tc.name FROM test_cases tc WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM test_runs tr WHERE tr.test_id=tc.test_id AND tr.status='failed') ORDER BY tc.name;",starter='SELECT name FROM test_cases;',tests='[{"name":"checkout"},{"name":"login"},{"name":"search"}]'),
('Why use EXISTS rather than a plain join for “has any failure”?',['To return every failure pair','To preserve one row per case','To sort automatically'],1,'EXISTS tests membership without duplicating the outer record.'),language='sql')
unit(5,'experiments','Confidence intervals, power, and A/B experiments',
'''A sample estimates a population; uncertainty depends on the sampling process and the independent unit. A 95% confidence procedure covers the fixed parameter in 95% of repeated samples under its assumptions. It does not assign 95% probability to this realized interval.\n\nA hypothesis test compares evidence with a specified null model. Type I error rejects a true null; Type II error misses a real effect. Statistical power depends on effect size, noise, sample size, and the decision threshold.''',
'''Pre-register the minimum practical improvement, dataset slices, randomization unit, stopping rule, and safety constraints. Use a paired test when both models answer the same cases; randomize users or sessions for an online A/B test when turns share context.\n\nFor a proportion, a Wilson interval is often more stable near zero or one than the normal approximation. For paired differences, resample independent case or conversation IDs. State the method and assumptions with the score.''',
'''Repeatedly testing many prompt variants until one appears significant raises false-positive risk. Looking only at overall mean hides subgroup regressions; correlation between prompt length and accuracy does not establish that longer prompts cause accuracy.\n\nCheck treatment assignment, missing outcomes, judge consistency, and sample ratio mismatch. Do not interpret an inconclusive result as proof of equivalence; define an equivalence margin and gather enough evidence for that question.''',
'''Evaluate the 82%-versus-85% claim using matched outcomes, confidence intervals, cost, latency, and critical risk slices. Report effect size in percentage points and say whether it crosses the predefined useful threshold.\n\nExperiment discipline is a release skill: an SDET must distinguish a lucky benchmark run from a robust improvement. Retain all variants and the untouched final dataset in the experiment lineage.''',
'import math\ndef wilson(successes,n,z=1.96):\n    if n <= 0: raise ValueError("positive sample size required")\n    p=successes/n\n    center=(p+z*z/(2*n))/(1+z*z/n)\n    half=z*math.sqrt(p*(1-p)/n+z*z/(4*n*n))/(1+z*z/n)\n    return center-half,center+half\nprint(wilson(85,100))',
py('Implement effect_pp(a, b): success proportions must be between 0 and 1; return B-A in percentage points.','def effect_pp(a,b):\n    if not (0 <= a <= 1 and 0 <= b <= 1): raise ValueError("invalid proportion")\n    return (b-a)*100','assert abs(effect_pp(.82,.85)-3)<1e-9\nassert abs(effect_pp(.9,.8)+10)<1e-9\ntry: effect_pp(-.1,.8)\nexcept ValueError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError("reject invalid proportions")','def effect_pp(a,b):\n    return 0'),
('A non-significant difference establishes what?',['Equivalence','Insufficient evidence under this test','No possible effect'],1,'Equivalence requires its own margin, assumptions, and adequate power.'))
